{%- comment -%}
  The theme's entire token layer, shared by every layout so a colour scheme
  behaves identically on the storefront, the password page and the gift card.

  Colour tokens are emitted once per scheme rather than once at :root, so any
  element carrying .color-scheme-2 rebases the whole palette for its subtree —
  including the derived accent ramp. Type and geometry stay global.

  The design's hand-picked camel ramp is regenerated from each scheme's single
  accent colour: steps 100-400 mix toward that scheme's own ground, steps
  600-900 step away from it. The weights are tuned to land on the original
  swatches when the accent is left at its default #8a6f52.

  Colour mode turns every scheme over rather than swapping in a second set of
  colours. The merchant keeps one palette and chooses which way up it is used,
  so a brand colour picked once is still the brand colour in either mode and
  there is no second set of swatches to keep in step. Ground and ink simply
  trade places — an exact swap, nothing guessed — and only the accents are
  derived, because an accent chosen to carry on paper will not carry on ink.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign dark = false
  if settings.color_mode == 'dark'
    assign dark = true
  endif
-%}

{%- for scheme in settings.color_schemes -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign raw_accent = scheme.settings.accent
    assign raw_accent_2 = scheme.settings.accent_2

    assign accent = raw_accent
    assign accent_2 = raw_accent_2
    assign button = scheme.settings.button
    assign button_label = scheme.settings.button_label
    assign button_hover = scheme.settings.button_hover

    unless dark
      comment
        Light is the shipped design, so nothing is derived here at all: the
        palette is used exactly as the merchant set it, as it always was.
      endcomment
      assign ground = scheme.settings.background
      assign ink = scheme.settings.text
      assign surface = scheme.settings.surface

      assign accent_600 = accent | color_darken: 6
      assign accent_700 = accent | color_darken: 14
      assign accent_800 = accent | color_darken: 21
      assign accent_900 = accent | color_darken: 29
      assign accent_2_700 = accent_2 | color_darken: 14
    else
      comment
        Ground and ink trade places outright. The surface is lifted off the new
        ground by the same small step the light schemes keep between their own
        background and surface, which is what makes image frames and panels
        read as a layer rather than as a block.
      endcomment
      assign ground = scheme.settings.text
      assign ink = scheme.settings.background
      assign surface = scheme.settings.text | color_lighten: 6

      comment
        Turning a scheme over changes which ground its accent has to sit on, so
        the accent is re-aimed at the ground it actually lands on rather than
        at the mode. That distinction is the whole thing: a scheme built dark
        to begin with — the one the design uses for its inverted sections —
        turns over into a *light* scheme, and treating it as dark because the
        shop is in dark mode leaves a pale accent on a pale ground.

        Perceived brightness decides both the direction and the distance, so
        this behaves the same for a navy as for a forest green.
      endcomment
      assign ground_bright = ground | color_brightness
      assign accent_bright = raw_accent | color_brightness
      assign accent_2_bright = raw_accent_2 | color_brightness

      if ground_bright < 128
        assign accent_lift = 23
        if accent_bright < 90
          assign accent_lift = 34
        elsif accent_bright > 170
          assign accent_lift = 0
        endif
        assign accent = raw_accent | color_lighten: accent_lift

        assign accent_2_lift = 18
        if accent_2_bright < 90
          assign accent_2_lift = 30
        elsif accent_2_bright > 170
          assign accent_2_lift = 0
        endif
        assign accent_2 = raw_accent_2 | color_lighten: accent_2_lift

        comment
          The ramp steps away from the ground. Darkening an accent that sits on
          a dark ground buries it, so here the upper steps lighten.
        endcomment
        assign accent_600 = accent | color_lighten: 6
        assign accent_700 = accent | color_lighten: 14
        assign accent_800 = accent | color_lighten: 21
        assign accent_900 = accent | color_lighten: 29
        assign accent_2_700 = accent_2 | color_lighten: 14
      else
        assign accent_drop = 0
        if accent_bright > 170
          assign accent_drop = 23
        elsif accent_bright > 128
          assign accent_drop = 10
        endif
        assign accent = raw_accent | color_darken: accent_drop

        assign accent_2_drop = 0
        if accent_2_bright > 170
          assign accent_2_drop = 18
        elsif accent_2_bright > 128
          assign accent_2_drop = 8
        endif
        assign accent_2 = raw_accent_2 | color_darken: accent_2_drop

        assign accent_600 = accent | color_darken: 6
        assign accent_700 = accent | color_darken: 14
        assign accent_800 = accent | color_darken: 21
        assign accent_900 = accent | color_darken: 29
        assign accent_2_700 = accent_2 | color_darken: 14
      endif

      comment
        A solid button is left alone when its colour can carry itself — a brand
        red is still the brand red in the dark. It is only turned over when it
        would land too close to the ground to be seen, which is what happens to
        a button that was taking the scheme's ink. Comparing the two
        brightnesses catches that in both directions: a dark button on a dark
        page, and the pale button of an inverted scheme on its new pale ground.
      endcomment
      assign button_gap = button | color_brightness | minus: ground_bright | abs
      if button_gap < 60
        assign button = ink
        assign button_label = ground
        assign button_hover = accent
      endif
    endunless

    comment
      What reads on top of an accent — the bag count, the sage badges. White in
      light mode, which is the shipped design. In dark mode the accents are
      lifted to carry on a dark ground, and white on a lifted accent is not
      readable, so the label instead takes whichever end of the scheme — its
      ground or its ink — stands furthest from the accent in brightness. Those
      two are always a light and a dark, so one of them always works.
    endcomment
    assign on_accent = '#ffffff'
    assign on_accent_2 = '#ffffff'

    if dark
      assign g_b = ground | color_brightness
      assign i_b = ink | color_brightness

      assign a_b = accent | color_brightness
      assign gap_ground = a_b | minus: g_b | abs
      assign gap_ink = a_b | minus: i_b | abs
      assign on_accent = ground
      if gap_ink > gap_ground
        assign on_accent = ink
      endif

      assign a2_b = accent_2 | color_brightness
      assign gap_ground_2 = a2_b | minus: g_b | abs
      assign gap_ink_2 = a2_b | minus: i_b | abs
      assign on_accent_2 = ground
      if gap_ink_2 > gap_ground_2
        assign on_accent_2 = ink
      endif
    endif

    comment
      Steps 100-400 mix toward whichever ground is in force, so the tint ramp
      stays quiet either way up.
    endcomment
    assign accent_100 = accent | color_mix: ground, 6
    assign accent_200 = accent | color_mix: ground, 20
    assign accent_300 = accent | color_mix: ground, 41
    assign accent_400 = accent | color_mix: ground, 65
  -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The *-rgb tokens must be comma separated, because the stylesheet feeds
    them to the legacy rgba() form: rgba(var(--color-text-rgb), 0.6).

    Liquid's `color.rgb` returns SPACE separated channels ("43 38 33"), which
    would produce `rgba(43 38 33, 0.6)` — invalid, and an invalid var()
    substitution makes the whole declaration compute to its initial value.
    That silently kills every divider, muted text colour, tint and ring in
    the theme. Emitting the channels individually is the only safe form.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% if forloop.first %}:root,{% endif %}
  .color-{{ scheme.id }} {
    --color-bg: {{ ground }};
    --color-bg-rgb: {{ ground.red }}, {{ ground.green }}, {{ ground.blue }};
    --color-surface: {{ surface }};
    --color-text: {{ ink }};
    --color-text-rgb: {{ ink.red }}, {{ ink.green }}, {{ ink.blue }};
    --color-divider: rgba(var(--color-text-rgb), {{ settings.divider_opacity | divided_by: 100.0 }});

    --color-accent: {{ accent }};
    --color-accent-rgb: {{ accent.red }}, {{ accent.green }}, {{ accent.blue }};
    --color-accent-100: {{ accent_100 }};
    --color-accent-200: {{ accent_200 }};
    --color-accent-300: {{ accent_300 }};
    --color-accent-400: {{ accent_400 }};
    --color-accent-500: {{ accent }};
    --color-accent-600: {{ accent_600 }};
    --color-accent-700: {{ accent_700 }};
    --color-accent-800: {{ accent_800 }};
    --color-accent-900: {{ accent_900 }};
    --color-accent-2: {{ accent_2 }};
    --color-accent-2-500: {{ accent_2 }};
    --color-accent-2-700: {{ accent_2_700 }};
    --on-accent: {{ on_accent }};
    --on-accent-2: {{ on_accent_2 }};

    {%- comment -%}
      Solid buttons carry their own colours per scheme rather than borrowing
      the text colour, so a merchant can make them their brand colour without
      dragging every heading along with it. Outline and underline buttons
      still follow the scheme's text colour, which is what makes them read as
      secondary.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    --color-button: {{ button }};
    --color-button-rgb: {{ button.red }}, {{ button.green }}, {{ button.blue }};
    --color-button-text: {{ button_label }};
    --color-button-hover: {{ button_hover }};

    --ink-60: rgba(var(--color-text-rgb), 0.6);
    --ink-70: rgba(var(--color-text-rgb), 0.7);
    --ink-80: rgba(var(--color-text-rgb), 0.8);
  }
{%- endfor -%}

:root {
  {%- comment -%}
    Tells the browser which way up the page is, so the parts it draws itself —
    scrollbars, select menus, date pickers, field spinners — come out to match
    instead of staying stubbornly white against a dark page.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  color-scheme: {% if dark %}dark{% else %}light{% endif %};

  --font-body: {{ settings.type_body_font.family }}, {{ settings.type_body_font.fallback_families }};
  --font-body-style: {{ settings.type_body_font.style }};
  --font-body-weight: {{ settings.type_body_font.weight }};
  --font-heading: {{ settings.type_heading_font.family }}, {{ settings.type_heading_font.fallback_families }};
  --font-heading-style: {{ settings.type_heading_font.style }};
  --font-accent: {{ settings.type_accent_font.family }}, {{ settings.type_accent_font.fallback_families }};
  --font-display-weight: {{ settings.heading_weight }};
  --type-scale: {{ settings.type_scale | divided_by: 100.0 }};
  --heading-tracking: {{ settings.heading_tracking | divided_by: 1000.0 }}em;

  --r: {{ settings.corner_radius }}px;
  --edge: clamp({{ settings.edge_min }}px, 4vw, {{ settings.edge_max }}px);
  --page-width: {{ settings.page_width }}px;
  --ease: cubic-bezier(.22, .61, .36, 1);
  --slow: cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1);
}
